WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.727
Your startup's real risk is not scale.

00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:09.454
It is corrupting your first users' data.

00:00:09.454 --> 00:00:13.180
So start boring: one managed Postgres node.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.907
Instagram rode it to a billion-dollar exit, and WhatsApp served nine hundred million users on it.

00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:20.331
When that one node finally strains, do you shard?

00:00:20.331 --> 00:00:23.756
Almost never.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:27.180
The first move is read replicas.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.604
All writes still go to one primary.

00:00:30.604 --> 00:00:34.028
Then you spread the reads across many copies.

00:00:34.028 --> 00:00:37.453
The primary streams its changes to them in the background, so a replica can lag by a few milliseconds.

00:00:37.453 --> 00:00:40.877
If a user must see their own write, send that one read back to the primary.

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:45.444
The classic mistake is over-engineering.

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:50.010
Sharding, microservices, web-scale NoSQL, all before you have a single user.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.577
Ship on one Postgres, and let real load tell you what to scale.

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:58.602
So: one boring Postgres node, then read replicas when reads pile up.

00:00:58.602 --> 00:01:02.626
That carries you well past a hundred thousand daily users.

00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:06.651
Next, how much that single node can actually do.
